    "case_id": "754",
    "case_summary": "A 3-year-and-9-month-old Chinese girl, first child of healthy nonconsanguineous parents (31-year-old mother and 54-year-old father). Birth weight 3130g, length 50cm, and OFC 34cm. Developmental milestones were comprehensively delayed: head control at 4 months, rolling over at 7 months, unable to walk, can only briefly stand with support. Speech significantly delayed. At 3 years 2 months: weight 10.5kg (<3rd percentile), height 92cm (3rd-10th percentile), OFC 45cm (<3rd percentile). Mental age evaluation at 3 years 6 months showed development equivalent to 5.7 months. History of congenital heart disease with corrective surgery at 54 days. First developed febrile seizures at age 2 years 7 months. Starting at age 3, experienced repeated right-sided epistaxis without obvious trauma, with bleeding episodes lasting 1.5 to 16 hours.\n\nPatient presents with:- Very low sloping forehead, wide nasal bridge, hypertelorism, thin upper lip, and micrognathia- Multiple small capillary malformations on trunk (hyperpigmented)- Short, tapered fingers- Clinodactyly of second toe bilaterally- Simian line- Erythema on forehead and right eyelid\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Hemoglobin: Dropped from 124 g/L to 64 g/L over three months- Platelet count: 116-166 × 109/L- Normal coagulation function and platelet function- Negative platelet antibody and autoantibody tests- Normal blood and urinary metabolic screeningImaging Studies:- Cranial CT at 2 years: No obvious abnormalities- Abdominal ultrasound: No definite vascular malformation- Contrast-enhanced paranasal sinus spiral CT: Abnormal enhancement of right inferior turbinate mucosa (CT values: 133 HU arterial phase, 159 HU venous phase)Other Tests:- EEG: Slowing of background rhythm (θ-δ) and epileptiform discharges in posterior brain region- Nasal endoscopic examination: Revealed vascular malformation with capillary dilation",
